WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.990 --> 00:00:02.910
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here today.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:07.980
And today I want to talk about what
is the hardest language to learn.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.640
Now, if you enjoy my
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:15.090
You can click on the bell in
order to get notifications.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:18.220
Uh, what's the hardest language to learn?

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:21.810
First of all, before I start, you may
notice there's some gaps in my library.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:25.710
I've been going through culling
some of the books that I no

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.570
longer want to keep around.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:32.250
So I've donated three boxes of
books to a local thrift store.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.230
Anyway, that's why it looks
a bit messy back there.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.670
Uh, the hardest language to learn,
you know, I think that's a very

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.450
difficult question because there are
so many factors that enter into that.

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.660
Um, you know, what do you
want to do with the language?

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.090
Uh, do you want to pass a test?

00:00:48.839 --> 00:00:50.790
Uh, do you want to just
enjoy the language?

00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.100
Do you want to enjoy movies,
books in the language?

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:55.740
Do you want to just be able to
communicate in the language.

00:00:55.740 --> 00:01:01.410
And different languages are, you know,
they have different degrees of difficulty

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.150
depending on what you want to achieve.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.690
But I would say that the biggest
factor in determining how difficult the

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:15.690
language is for you is how motivated
you are to learn that language.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:21.780
If you find your motivation flagging,
if you find that you don't like the

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.085
language, that's going to make that
language very difficult to learn.

00:01:26.805 --> 00:01:29.994
If on the other hand, you love
the language you want to...

00:01:29.994 --> 00:01:31.635
learn the language,

00:01:31.635 --> 00:01:37.125
you either you have a friend or a loved
one or a relative, or you enjoy aspects

00:01:37.125 --> 00:01:42.015
of their culture and you're very motivated
that language will be easier to learn

00:01:42.015 --> 00:01:47.565
because any objective difficulties only
make the language more interesting.

00:01:47.565 --> 00:01:51.375
So you'll be, you know, pushing
yourself to overcome these difficulties.

00:01:51.375 --> 00:01:57.255
So the number one consideration
in my mind is, you know, your

00:01:57.255 --> 00:01:58.395
relationship to the language.

00:01:59.195 --> 00:02:04.414
Second thing is how easy is it
to find content of interest?

00:02:04.414 --> 00:02:09.595
Content that's comprehensible maybe,
but potentially even if it's not yet

00:02:09.704 --> 00:02:14.105
comprehensible, if it's attractive
to you, and this is very subjective.

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:19.560
So here again, the, some people are
attracted by say anime for Japanese,

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.120
which doesn't appeal to me at all.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.120
Uh, it can be anything.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:25.710
I happen to be attracted to history.

00:02:25.950 --> 00:02:31.440
So, uh, and, and right now I'm very
interested in the sort of central Asia,

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:38.100
uh, Iran and, you know, Turkish, Arabic,
even, uh, just finished reading a book

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:44.870
about, uh Genghis Khan and the Mongols and
the amazing impact they had on history,

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:46.970
both in a negative and a positive way.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:47.090
Yeah.

00:02:47.445 --> 00:02:52.425
So those kinds of things that, that, um,
you know, make it a, you know, in terms

00:02:52.425 --> 00:02:57.405
of something you can sink your teeth into:
literature, history, music, whatever.

00:02:57.555 --> 00:02:58.905
That's got to be there.

00:02:59.325 --> 00:03:02.415
Uh, where you can't find that,
then it's more difficult.

00:03:02.835 --> 00:03:06.930
And this can depend, this can vary
from, you know, time to time, you

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:12.000
may be trying to learn a language
and not find anything of interest.

00:03:12.209 --> 00:03:16.440
And then later on, because you meet a
friend or because you discover a movie

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:20.340
or something, all of a sudden that
interest, then, you know, you're, you're

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:22.170
fired up again to learn the language.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:29.070
And that's why it's so useful now
that in addition to whatever, you

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.140
know, beginner content you use.

00:03:31.739 --> 00:03:37.700
You know, Assimil or Teach Yourself
or mini-stories at LingQ, uh, that,

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:42.690
that then you move on to things that
you can find on the internet and

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:44.079
which you can bring in to LingQ.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:48.630
For example, whether it be off, uh,
you know, from YouTube or, or, or, uh,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:54.810
eBooks, which I have often used, or,
uh, you know, a Netflix, uh, you have

00:03:54.810 --> 00:04:01.350
to, you know, make it, you know, make
yourself, put yourself in a position

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:06.570
where you can access things that might
really, you know, turn your crank.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.300
And there's the expression in French...

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.000
give yourself a chance to get
carried away by the language.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:16.920
Don't go in with too many prejudices.

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:19.680
Saying this language is no good.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:21.300
I don't like the sound of the language.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:22.380
I find it boring.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:27.800
Get in there, start to discover things
and pretty soon you may find that, uh, you

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.900
know, you will get attracted, uh, by it.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:33.590
You know, I'm, I'm now going after
Persian and Arabic and Turkish, which

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:37.190
I wasn't particularly interested in
before and now I'm very interested in.

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.860
So that element of
motivation is very important.

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:47.100
Of course, you know, if we look at
objective considerations, Um, the more,

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:52.050
the language is similar to a language
you know, the easier it's going to be.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.300
The more the vocabulary is similar,
particularly the vocabulary, so that you

00:04:57.300 --> 00:04:59.010
don't have to struggle to remember words.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:03.719
And I always say that the number of
words you know, uh, your, the size

00:05:03.719 --> 00:05:07.200
of your vocabulary will determine
your ability to understand.

00:05:07.469 --> 00:05:11.130
If you can understand well, you'll
eventually learn to speak well,

00:05:11.790 --> 00:05:13.500
uh, or at least to communicate.

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:19.440
So, uh, how similar the language is to a
language you already know is a big factor.

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.900
This can have pluses and minuses.

00:05:22.140 --> 00:05:27.555
Now I'm finding, for example, in
Portuguese, that I know Spanish, therefore

00:05:27.555 --> 00:05:33.825
Portuguese, uh, from one perspective
is easier because the vocabulary

00:05:33.825 --> 00:05:35.885
is like more than 90%, the same.

00:05:36.215 --> 00:05:40.534
And the grammatical structure is
more than 90% the same, but because

00:05:40.534 --> 00:05:45.425
they're so similar, it's difficult
to totally throw myself into

00:05:45.425 --> 00:05:47.164
sort of a Portuguese environment.

00:05:47.555 --> 00:05:53.675
The Spanish control center in my brain
is kind of holding on and preventing

00:05:53.675 --> 00:05:58.500
me from, you know, totally starting
a fresh in this other language.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:03.030
On the other hand, if I go into Arabic
or Persian, there is no other language

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:05.790
that is influencing my Persian or Arabic.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:11.610
So to some degree, that is a difficulty
with languages that are very similar,

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:14.040
but that's a minor consideration.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:18.870
Overwhelmingly the more similar
the languages are, the easier

00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:20.400
it's going to be to learn them.

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:23.830
Another consideration of
course, is the writing system.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.900
Uh, I've grown up reading,
using the Latin alphabet.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:32.820
So even though I can read the
Cyrillic alphabet, I can read Chinese

00:06:32.820 --> 00:06:39.510
characters, hangul, kanna, even
Greek, Arabic with some difficulty,

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:41.940
you know, it's so difficult.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:47.040
I find to get used to comfortably reading
in another script it's one thing to

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.790
decipher it, figure out this is, these
are the symbols, this is what they, you

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.290
know, the sounds that they represent.

00:06:52.695 --> 00:06:55.844
But to actually get there where you
can just comfortably read in another

00:06:55.844 --> 00:06:57.674
script, I find that's a big step.

00:06:58.575 --> 00:07:02.474
Like for me, Polish and Czech are
much easier than Russian and Ukrainian

00:07:02.505 --> 00:07:04.515
because it's so much easier for me.

00:07:04.544 --> 00:07:07.125
There's no resistance when I
read in the Latin alphabet.

00:07:07.155 --> 00:07:10.965
Whereas reading in Cyrillic, even though
I have read so much in Cyrillic, like

00:07:11.174 --> 00:07:12.794
it's just that little bit more difficult.

00:07:12.945 --> 00:07:14.955
So it's always a challenge.

00:07:14.984 --> 00:07:19.215
And when I learn another language with
another script, I put a lot of effort

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:24.090
into just, just making sure I spend
enough time reading in that script because

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.010
that's going to improve my comprehension.

00:07:26.505 --> 00:07:30.614
And that's why I, for the time being,
I've put Turkish on the back burner

00:07:30.885 --> 00:07:36.344
and I'm focusing on Persian and Arabic
in order to get my brain more and more

00:07:36.344 --> 00:07:38.814
used to reading in this other script.

00:07:38.955 --> 00:07:43.635
And I know from experience that everything
in language learning, including reading

00:07:43.755 --> 00:07:49.125
in another script is just a question of
time and enough exposure so that your

00:07:49.125 --> 00:07:51.554
brain will gradually develop new habits.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.570
But the emphasis is on
gradually and slowly.

00:07:54.659 --> 00:07:56.460
There's no instant Eureka!

00:07:56.670 --> 00:08:02.159
So a different writing system obviously
is an element of difficulty, in

00:08:02.159 --> 00:08:06.420
particular a writing system like Chinese
characters where it's not phonetic.

00:08:06.690 --> 00:08:12.270
It's not a matter of learning a finite
number of symbols, 20, 30, 40, 50 symbols.

00:08:12.659 --> 00:08:16.320
You have to learn thousands of pictograms.

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:19.260
So that makes Chinese more difficult.

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:23.609
I've said before that there is a
compensation because so many of what

00:08:23.609 --> 00:08:28.440
we would term words in English are
combinations of these pictograms.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:33.419
So once you have them, you can then
easily, more easily, remember new words

00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:37.890
that are combinations of these characters
that you have already learned, but

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:40.169
still it does make it more difficult.

00:08:41.295 --> 00:08:43.365
Pronunciation can be an issue.

00:08:43.785 --> 00:08:49.005
Uh, but to me, that's also a question
of time and, uh, you know, the

00:08:49.005 --> 00:08:53.505
solution to many of these difficulties
is simply exposing yourself to the

00:08:53.505 --> 00:08:57.435
language, listening enough, getting
the intonation of the language.

00:08:57.735 --> 00:09:00.885
Speaking, without feeling
self-conscious about it.

00:09:01.785 --> 00:09:06.945
Uh, and of course, grammar, I find that,
uh, you know, objectively speaking,

00:09:07.305 --> 00:09:13.215
uh, Chinese and even Japanese and
Korean grammar are less complicated.

00:09:13.215 --> 00:09:17.865
There are fewer little bits of
detail that you can easily get

00:09:17.865 --> 00:09:23.730
wrong than say in Russian, Slavic
languages, German and so forth.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:29.310
So the more details there are in the
language, the more things that change

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:34.620
like case endings change or conjugations,
the verb endings change, the more

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:39.420
of these things that are not so much
a question of logic of the language

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:43.860
-
but I mean, there is that logic,
but a logic that is hidden behind

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:48.000
a bunch of rules and tables - the
more of these things you have in a

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.560
language, the more difficult it is.

00:09:50.775 --> 00:09:52.755
Especially to speak correctly.

00:09:52.875 --> 00:09:57.075
Now, if your objective is to
understand the language, to be able

00:09:57.075 --> 00:10:01.935
to communicate, connect with people
as is the case with me and most of

00:10:01.935 --> 00:10:07.005
my languages, I'm not tremendously
concerned about how accurately I speak.

00:10:07.245 --> 00:10:10.995
Of course, I want to speak more
accurately, but, but I can't

00:10:10.995 --> 00:10:13.785
speak accurately in all of these
languages that I'm learning.

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:18.120
Uh, I want to speak more accurately
if I make a video in Arabic.

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:22.699
And I, you know, I, I say, uh,
that I, you know, ... instead

00:10:23.189 --> 00:10:26.699
of ... like you've got to change
the beginning, the suffix, depending

00:10:26.699 --> 00:10:28.260
on the person and stuff like that.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:30.839
And I get it wrong some of the
time, right some of the time.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:35.969
And I hear that and I notice it and
slowly, hopefully I improve, but

00:10:35.969 --> 00:10:39.300
it doesn't, uh, in any way, detract
from my enjoyment of the language.

00:10:39.900 --> 00:10:44.310
On the other hand, if your goal, if
you're working uh in Arabic or Russian

00:10:44.325 --> 00:10:48.345
or whatever, and you're, uh, putting
out business correspondence or you're

00:10:48.345 --> 00:10:51.945
at formal meetings and there's obviously
a, or you're going to write a test,

00:10:51.975 --> 00:10:54.885
then there is a greater
need to be accurate.

00:10:55.335 --> 00:10:59.925
So difficulty in terms of understanding
it is one thing, difficulty in terms of

00:10:59.925 --> 00:11:04.005
being able to read it and listen to it
and enjoy movies is one thing, difficulty

00:11:04.005 --> 00:11:09.045
in terms of being accurate and creating
a very positive impression because you

00:11:09.045 --> 00:11:11.325
are essentially making very few mistakes,

00:11:11.775 --> 00:11:13.335
that's another level of difficulty.

00:11:13.635 --> 00:11:18.375
I think, uh, honestly, uh, you know,
in French, some people find French

00:11:18.375 --> 00:11:22.455
difficult to understand because of
the way the words flow into each

00:11:22.455 --> 00:11:24.194
other with the sort of liaison.

00:11:24.765 --> 00:11:28.895
Uh, but another big obstacle
in French is the gender.

00:11:29.295 --> 00:11:32.235
Gender is more obvious in Spanish.

00:11:32.834 --> 00:11:37.395
Uh, you know, most words that end
in "o" masculine and most words

00:11:37.395 --> 00:11:38.834
that end in "a" are feminine.

00:11:38.895 --> 00:11:40.305
Spanish, fairly straightforward...

00:11:40.305 --> 00:11:40.745
Italian.

00:11:41.115 --> 00:11:42.015
French, not so.

00:11:42.525 --> 00:11:47.635
And when I listen to our Canadian
politicians, uh, English-speaking

00:11:47.655 --> 00:11:50.265
politicians who speak French and
some of them speak quite well.

00:11:50.415 --> 00:11:54.704
And yet the gender is the thing
that trips them up because it's not

00:11:54.704 --> 00:11:55.995
something that we have in English.

00:11:56.415 --> 00:11:58.694
It's very difficult to develop new habits.

00:11:59.085 --> 00:12:00.255
New grammatical habits.

00:12:00.495 --> 00:12:02.385
It doesn't prevent them
from communicating.

00:12:03.555 --> 00:12:07.465
And the similarity with people
learning English, uh, I often mention

00:12:07.485 --> 00:12:12.685
that we have a colleague Russian who
speaks absolutely perfect English.

00:12:12.685 --> 00:12:17.055
There isn't anything he can't
express or explain in English.

00:12:17.265 --> 00:12:21.285
And yet to him, the articles remain
a problem because obviously it's not

00:12:21.285 --> 00:12:26.069
something that is part of the sort of
language habits of Russian speakers, or

00:12:26.069 --> 00:12:29.970
for that matter speakers of many other
languages that don't have articles.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:34.439
Uh, there are languages with no articles,
languages with no gender, languages with

00:12:34.439 --> 00:12:39.020
no, uh, with, uh, no singular or plural.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:41.870
Um, yeah.

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:46.280
Um, languages, uh, have a different,
uh, different ways of expressing

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:47.510
essentially the same thoughts.

00:12:47.930 --> 00:12:49.550
And we have to get used to them.

00:12:50.025 --> 00:12:53.895
And, uh, some of them are a little
harder to, you know, develop

00:12:53.925 --> 00:12:56.595
these new habits than in others.

00:12:57.135 --> 00:12:58.155
So there you have it.

00:12:58.155 --> 00:13:02.865
So I don't want to say that any particular
language is harder to learn, but I

00:13:02.915 --> 00:13:07.275
rather want it to set out what I think
are some of the factors that determine

00:13:07.485 --> 00:13:10.515
how difficult a language may be for you.

00:13:11.385 --> 00:13:11.805
Okay.

00:13:12.105 --> 00:13:14.235
That's all I want him
to say on that subject.

00:13:14.265 --> 00:13:14.835
Bye for now.

